WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.829
It's easy to spot fake news.

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:07.000
It's kind of easy to spot when you see it,
'cause a lot of it is very click-baitish.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.820
There'st he kind of fake news that's like
a blown up picture of Owen Wilson,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.280
it's like 'you won't believe how much weight
this actor has lost.'

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.540
We quizzed people on Venice Beach to see if
they really could pick out fake news headlines

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.940
like this one: 'Breaking News: Hillary Clinton
Killed During FBI Raid.'

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.360
That's false!

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.700
Trump Tower opens in Pyongyang, the capitol
of North Korea.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.900
Yeah that's fake news.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.820
Everyone we spoke to spotted those bogus headlines
easily.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.260
But what about this one, from the progressive
news site Salon.com?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.920
Donald Trump threatens to deport NFL players
who defy him and that's just the beginning.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.960
He probably would say something super crazy
and dumb like that.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.790
A lot of stuff has been happening with the
NFL lately and Trump

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.060
so I mean, and he does, I feel like he does
have the power, it could be true, yeah.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:03.240
That's like a fake news headline, like he
said something along the lines of, like,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.560
that someone could like extrapolate that from
whatever his tweet said,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.500
but I don't think that's technically true.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.420
That's pretty much what happened.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.060
Trump said that players who don't kneel for
the national anthem

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.700
'maybe shouldn't be in this country'

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.400
and Salon took the logical leap to infer that
the president wanted to deport

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.620
insufficiently respectful players

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.350
but what about misleading headlines that come
from mainstream sources, like CNN?

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.180
Trump asks Japan to build cars in the U.S.
it already does.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.380
True.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.420
Yeah I did hear about that, I read about it.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.980
He may have heard about it on CNN, which later
corrected the headline

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.640
to reflect the fact Trump said he wanted Japan
to build more cars in America.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.980
Given that, would you consider that headline
to be fake, misleading?

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.360
It's just a little bit misleading.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.380
Yeah the way it's phrased makes it sound like
he's so dumb.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.240
As the media environment continues to fracture,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.820
it's natural for consumers to gravitate to
sources they trust and ignore ones they don't.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.660
But even respectable news sources often deliver
factual information subtly blended with commentary.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.340
Take this Politico headline which states the
fact that the Fed raised rates

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.840
paired with an editorial comment that such
a move signals the strength of Trump's economy.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.720
this news/commentary hybrid led some of our
interviewees

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.580
to believe the headline came from a pro-Trump
source.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.420
I feel like that would be more not a mainstream
media source though.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.620
I feel like it'd be Fox News.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.620
It's actually Politico.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:35.260
Politico?

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:35.920
Yeah.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.480
And then there's the confounding fact that
partisan sources

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.780
occasionally report factual information

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.580
such as this fairly straightforward headline

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.200
about the content of recently revealed text messages

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.120
sent by an FBI agent high up in the Clinton
email server and Trump/Russia investigations.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.360
Justice Department IG Report Says Peter Strzok
Had 'Biased State Of Mind'

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.480
Do you think that that sounds like a legit
headline?

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.640
Uhm yeah that sounds legit, what website is
that on?

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.180
That's from Breitbart, do you consider them
legitimate?

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.480
No it's awful!

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.140
Yeah so definitely screwed up on that one.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.500
So what's the news consumer to do in this
confusing environment?

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.520
One proposed solution is the regulation of
fake news.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.980
Congress held a hearing on just this matter
on October of 2017

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.020
with senators threatening the social media
giants to step up or be regulated.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:28.720
You have to be the ones to do something about
it, uhm or we will.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.220
French politicians have begun debating legislation

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.340
that would give judges the ability to block
what they deem to be fake news

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.280
in the months leading up to an election.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.900
I think that infringes a little bit,

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:43.980
I would be surprised if judges in France can
review every news story possible.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.240
I mean that just sounds crazy.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.420
But Americans have proven much more skeptical

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.200
with polls showing a strong majority disagreeing
with government regulation.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.800
Do you think the government should step in
and regulate it?

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.660
Nah 'cause the government is corrupt and every
time they touch something

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.140
it just goes like, like completely wrong.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.440
You can say that government can play a role
in it

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.060
but then that kind of breaks, you know, freedom
of speech.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.420
Do you guys think that social media companies
like Facebook or Twitter

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.690
should do something to stem the spread of
fake news

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.760
or should they control it or regulate it in
some way?

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.140
I mean they should but the issue there is
bias.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.040
Americans have been slightly more open to
the idea of tech companies policing fake news

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.280
which wouldn't violate the First Amendment.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.200
In general do you trust companies like Facebook
to be transparent and have accountability?

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.760
I do, yeah I do.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.260
But there's a generational divide on the question,

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.700
with younger Americans being more skeptical
of tech giants regulating speech.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.920
Do you trust those companies to be transparent
and accountable?

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.980
Of course not, no, what are you talking about?  No!

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.340
And perhaps in that generational divide lies
an answer,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.680
younger Americans don't know anything else,
a fractured media environment

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.610
is the reality into which they were born.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.700
They don't really watch the news, they're
not on in the morning watching Fox, like they're not!

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.680
Yeah no one watches TV anymore, nobody watches
TV anymore.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:11.620
I don't go on Facebook as much anymore, like
Facebook is now getting for like older people.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.840
Much concern about fake news is ultimately
rooted in a fear of echo chambers or filter bubbles

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.660
that cut off consumers from diverse viewpoints.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.139
But that's an overblown fear with recent studies
showing

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.120
that most Americans are in fact exposed to
a diversity of viewpoints.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.440
The echo chamber panic may ironically be largely
the result of an echo chamber of political journalists

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.520
telling each other it's a big problem.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:44.300
We're more opposed to censorship I think because
we're so used to the variety of opinions online.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.500
Media literacy and critical thinking skills

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.560
are the most valuable weapons for combating fake news

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.580
and Generation Z just may be more equipped
to navigate the complicated media landscape

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.729
than are Baby Boomers, Gen Xers or even Millenials.

00:05:56.729 --> 00:06:00.780
The Baby Boomers don't understand it, you
know, and the Millennial kind of understand it

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.340
but they haven't, they aren't used to it in
the same way.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:09.540
We're more able to do our own fact checking
and more willing to

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.400
whereas like my mom clicks on something and
is like

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.699
'this is the only article about this topic,
wow, I can't believe I found this!'

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.280
Owen Wilson really got fat.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.320
He really did, look at him!

